	<h1>Meet our President</h1>
	
	<p>Ellen L. Brothers is president of American Girl and executive vice president of Mattel, as well as a member of Mattel&#8217;s management committee. As president of American Girl, Brothers is responsible for the strategic vision and day-to-day operations of the organization, which include direct marketing, experiential retail, and publishing channels. </p>
 
<p>Brothers joined American Girl, which is headquartered in Middleton, WI, in 1995 as vice president of Catalogue Marketing. She was named president of the company in 2000, succeeding founder Pleasant T. Rowland. Under Brothers&#8217; direction, American Girl has grown to become one of the top 25 consumer cataloguers in the country and recently expanded into the entertainment industry with several television movies and an upcoming feature film release. In addition, American Girl is one of the nation&#8217;s leading children&#8217;s publishers and experiential retailers, with its renowned American Girl Place destinations in Chicago, New York, and Los Angeles.</p>
 
<p>A 1978 graduate of the University of Illinois, Brothers has spent her entire career in the direct-marketing industry. One of her most notable past affiliations was with Pepperidge Farm Mail Order Company in Connecticut, where she served as president and general manager for the last three of her ten years with the organization.</p>
 
<p>Brothers has been a member of the Catalogue and Marketing Councils of the Direct Marketing Association (DMA) for the past 17 years and served on the DMA&#8217;s board of directors from 1997 through 2000. Brothers is also a past president of the New England Mail Order Association, the largest association of cataloguers in the country, and she was selected as one of Target Marketing magazine&#8217;s &#8220;200 Most Influential Direct Marketers&#8221; for three years in a row. She currently serves on the board of directors of the Children Affected by AIDS Foundation (CAAF) and Kids In Distressed Situations (K.I.D.S.). Brothers is also a member of the Committee of 200, an organization of leading women entrepreneurs and corporate business executives in the United States, and she oversees American Girl&#8217;s Fund for Children, the company&#8217;s grant program that has awarded more than $7 million to local children&#8217;s charities since its inception in 1992. </p>
